Troubleshooting
Model accuracy is low (<70%)

Increase training data samples in generate_logs.py
Adjust alert condition thresholds
Retrain with more balanced data
No alerts detected during monitoring

Lower alert_threshold in LogMonitor (try 0.5)
Verify live_logs.json contains alert conditions
Check model loaded correctly
Import errors

Ensure virtual environment is activated: source venv/bin/activate
Reinstall packages: pip install scikit-learn pandas numpy
File not found errors

Run scripts from ~/predictive-alerts directory
Verify all data files were generated successfully
Conclusion
You have successfully built a predictive alert system that:

Trains a machine learning model on historical log data
Extracts meaningful features from system logs
Predicts potential alerts before they become critical
Monitors live logs and triggers alerts based on predictions
Provides a foundation for proactive system monitoring
This approach enables proactive incident management by predicting issues before they escalate, reducing downtime and improving system reliability. The techniques learned here apply to various monitoring scenarios including application performance, security threats, and infrastructure health.

Key Takeaways:

Feature engineering is critical for log analysis
Random Forest classifiers work well for alert prediction
Real-time monitoring requires efficient data processing
Model accuracy depends on quality training data
